      "MAIN_bytes": 11952,
      "MAIN_SHA256": "bb58ccd950759a586bcea7ccdbadf714e1814ddeeb1f207c8a46a4ae83dc3832",
      "title": "An explicit counterexample to three-summand Kato-kernel splitting",
      "SEO_title": "An explicit counterexample to three-summand Kato-kernel splitting | Alec Kriebel",
      "description": "An exponent-two characteristic-two example disproves three-summand Kato-kernel splitting at n=1; the n=0 boundary remains positive.",
      "rights": "Authored paper, code and analysis CC BY 4.0; third-party rights retained",
      "abstract": "Question 2 in Laghribi’s contribution to Oberwolfach Report 5/2016 asks whether a mixed Kato kernel is the sum of the kernels of a finite purely inseparable extension, a separable quadratic extension, and a bilinear Pfister function field. We give an explicit negative instance in H₂², using an exponent-two extension and a binary bilinear Pfister form that remains anisotropic. The mixed class is exactly a specialization of the generators of Aravire–Laghribi–O’Ryan (2019/2020). We prove directly that it remains outside the sum after the additional Pfister kernel is included, by a universal norm and residue obstruction. The original degree-one cohomology case is separately positive. No firstness claim is made.",
      "native_version": "1.0.0",
      "publication_date": "2026-10-17",
      "DOI": "10.5281/zenodo.23444050",
      "source_schema": {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
        "name": "An explicit counterexample to three-summand Kato-kernel splitting",
        "headline": "An explicit counterexample to three-summand Kato-kernel splitting",
        "description": "An exponent-two characteristic-two example disproves three-summand Kato-kernel splitting at n=1; the n=0 boundary remains positive.",
        "abstract": "Question 2 in Laghribi’s contribution to Oberwolfach Report 5/2016 asks whether a mixed Kato kernel is the sum of the kernels of a finite purely inseparable extension, a separable quadratic extension, and a bilinear Pfister function field. We give an explicit negative instance in H₂², using an exponent-two extension and a binary bilinear Pfister form that remains anisotropic. The mixed class is exactly a specialization of the generators of Aravire–Laghribi–O’Ryan (2019/2020). We prove directly that it remains outside the sum after the additional Pfister kernel is included, by a universal norm and residue obstruction. The original degree-one cohomology case is separately positive. No firstness claim is made.",
